[38;2;124;124;124mwhether the $\langle M \rangle$ condition alone suffices.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAnd the answer is YES, because the Itô isometry directly uses $\langle M [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\rangle$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$E[(H \bullet M)_t^2] = E\left[\int_0^t H^2 d\langle M \rangle\right]$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo if $E[\int_0^\infty H^2 d\langle M \rangle] < \infty$, then $\sup_t [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mE[(H\bullet M)_t^2] \leq E[\int_0^\infty H^2 d\langle M \rangle] < \infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis means $H \bullet M$ is square integrable.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write a clean proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me also think about whether there could be a counterexample. Could[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthere be a situation where $E[\int H^2 d\langle M \rangle] < \infty$ but $H [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\bullet M$ is NOT square integrable?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Itô isometry is a theorem, not a definition. Let me recall the precise [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstatement.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $M$ a square integrable martingale (i.e., $M \in \mathcal{M}^2$, meaning $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\sup_t E[M_t^2] < \infty$ and $M$ is a martingale), and $H$ a predictable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprocess with $\|H\|_{L^2(M)}^2 := E[\int_0^\infty H^2 d\langle M \rangle] < [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\infty$, the stochastic integral $H \bullet M$ is well-defined and belongs to $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{M}^2$, with:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$E[(H \bullet M)_\infty^2] = E\left[\int_0^\infty H^2 d\langle M [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\rangle\right]$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is the fundamental isometry of the stochastic integral. It's proved by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfirst establishing it for simple predictable processes and then extending by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdensity.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is definitively YES.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also consider: is there any issue with the definition of $H \bullet M$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mitself? The stochastic integral $H \bullet M$ is defined for $H$ in $L^2(M) := [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\{H \text{ predictable} : E[\int_0^\infty H^2 d\langle M \rangle] < \infty\}$. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSo the condition $E[\int_0^\infty H^2 d\langle M \rangle] < \infty$ is exactly [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe condition needed to define the stochastic integral in the $L^2$ theory, and [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe isometry guarantees the result is in $\mathcal{M}^2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the proof is straightforward:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m1. $M$ is a square integrable martingale, so $M \in \mathcal{M}^2$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. $H$ is predictable with $E[\int_0^\infty H^2 d\langle M \rangle] < \infty$, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mso $H \in L^2(M)$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. By the Itô isometry for square integrable martingales, $H \bullet M \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{M}^2$ with $E[(H\bullet M)_\infty^2] = E[\int_0^\infty H^2 d\langle M [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\rangle] < \infty$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. In particular, $\sup_t E[(H\bullet M)_t^2] \leq E[(H\bullet M)_\infty^2] < [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\infty$ (since $(H\bullet M)_t^2 \leq (H\bullet M)_\infty^2$ is NOT true in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgeneral... wait, actually for a martingale, by Jensen's inequality, $E[(H\bullet[0m[38;2;124;124;124mM)_t^2] \leq E[(H\bullet M)_\infty^2]$ since $(H\bullet M)_t = E[(H\bullet [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mM)_\infty | \mathcal{F}_t]$ and squaring is convex).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, more directly: $(H\bullet M)_t = E[(H\bullet M)_\infty | \mathcal{F}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m_t]$ (since $H\bullet M$ is a uniformly integrable martingale when it's in $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{M}^2$), so by Jensen:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$E[(H\bullet M)_t^2] = E[E[(H\bullet M)_\infty | \mathcal{F}_t]^2] \leq [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mE[(H\bullet M)_\infty^2]$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\sup_t E[(H\bullet M)_t^2] \leq E[(H\bullet M)_\infty^2] = E[\int_0^\infty [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mH^2 d\langle M \rangle] < \infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore $H \bullet M$ is square integrable.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the complete proof now.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I should also address the relationship between the two conditions more[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcarefully, and explain why the $\langle M \rangle$ condition is actually the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmore natural one.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key insight is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The Itô isometry naturally involves $\langle M \rangle$, not $[M]$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $E[\int H^2 d[M]]$ and $E[\int H^2 d\langle M \rangle]$ are generally [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdifferent (the difference is a local martingale that may not be a true [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmartingale).[0m
